# 0.3.6 - 2026-10-30

Adds separate copyable links for this device, the local network, and Tailscale. The network link
listener starts only when requested. Devices on 0.3.5 and 0.3.6 work together.

## Added

- File actions can copy a link addressed to the LAN IP or the device's Tailscale IP, as well as
  the existing device-local link. The URL streams through the same capability-protected media
  endpoint; it works while NomadFS is running on the device that created it.

# 0.3.5 - 2026-10-30

A maintenance release: no change in how NomadFS behaves. Devices on 0.3.4 and 0.3.5 work
together.

## Changed

- The app's device list and mount start only shape what the core answers: the core formats
  device fingerprints and waits for a mount to appear.

## For developers

- Discovery's membership news and relaying no longer import the DHT node: they are
  `Gossip(Node)` and `Relay(Node)`, so the files depend on each other one way.
- The last repeated code went: one `~` expansion (`env.expandHome`), one peer sample for the
  gossip sweeps, one test store helper. `zig build atlas`: 0 errors, 2 warnings.

# 0.3.4 - 2026-10-30

Folder backups now go into the swarm folder, and this release takes in a full code review:
two data-loss bugs, a crash and a long list of reliability fixes. Devices on 0.3.3 and 0.3.4
work together; update them all for the relay and sync fixes.

## Changed

- **Backed-up folders are in the swarm folder.** A folder a device marks for backup (a phone's
  Camera) appears there as `<device>/<folder>/`, each file with its own date, and every backup
  device keeps it like any swarm file: stored once, nothing copied on the phone. It's a backup,
  not a mirror: freeing space on the phone keeps the photos in the swarm; a photo deleted or
  moved in the swarm folder isn't added back. The separate "(backup)" folders are gone; a backup
  device moves the ones it kept into the swarm folder (deleted files included) on its first start.
- **Files keep their original dates.** Adding a file keeps its modified time, and times set
  through the mount (`touch`, `cp -p`, `rsync -t`/`-a`) are stored and reach every device, so
  sorting by date follows the photos, not the order they were copied.
- **Tickets and invites list every address** (LAN and VPNs such as Tailscale) from the app and
  the command line, so pairing works away from home too.
- **A quiet swarm sends nothing for the swarm folder**: devices announce their folder's digest
  in the pings they already exchange and fetch a manifest only when it differs.

## Added

- `nomadfs --join <code>` joins a swarm from the command line, the same way the app does.
- The swarm folder shows when a device's changes couldn't be synced ("N couldn't sync").

## Fixed

- **Renaming a folder onto a folder with files in it deleted its contents on every device.** It
  now fails with "directory not empty", as on any disk.
- **Devices reached only through a relay stayed unreachable from outside** (a home server whose
  link to the relay was itself relayed); the app away from home saw only the relay. Direct
  connections are preferred and retried.
- The app crashed when a mount failed.
- Swarm folders stopped syncing once their manifest passed 10 MiB (about 11k-37k files); the
  limit is now the manifest's own, 64 MiB.
- One slow or offline device no longer freezes the app: previews, downloads, browsing and the
  device list don't hold its lock while they wait, and the device list asks every device at once.
- A device that answers on no address isn't redialed by every caller (up to 2 minutes).
- Revoking a device from the command line while a node runs now closes its connections, as the
  app's revoke does; a revocation from another device also stops reads from going to it.
- Two nodes can no longer run on the same configuration (the app and `nomadfs --mount`, say);
  the second one says so instead of overwriting the first one's files.
- A rotated swarm key is adopted by nodes run from the command line too (they kept the old key),
  with or without `--config`.
- The mobile API saves the read cache limit; the File Details view finds a file a backup device
  holds; a swarm conflict copy always gets a valid name.
- Hardening against misbehaving peers: bounded walks of their file trees, per-message size limits
  before reading, a cap on peers asked per DHT lookup, downloads stopped at the listed size, and
  a deadline on router (UPnP) replies.
- Shutdown stops every background thread before freeing what it uses.

## For developers

- One core service per feature for the command line, the app and the mobile API (membership,
  settings, shares, backups, file reads, key rotation, joining); `zig build atlas` checks the
  layers (0 errors); `fs` reaches the network only through `cluster`; discovery's gossip and
  relaying are in their own files; shared transport and storage helpers replace their copies.

# 0.3.0 - 2026-10-29

A clean break: **every device needs 0.3.0**. The app has a new ID
(`com.foreachdeveloper.nomadfs`): on Android, uninstall the old NomadFS, install this one and
pair the phone again; on the desktop, install it from the release (installed 0.2.x apps won't
offer this update). Devices on 0.2.x can't sync the swarm folder with 0.3.0 devices.

## Added

- **Reads from every device that has the file, at once.** A new distribution layer picks, for
  each piece of a file, the device expected to answer first, by measured speed and current
  load: a stream draws from every holder, mostly the fastest, and keeps going when one is slow
  or goes away. A device that doesn't have the file is remembered, not asked for every piece.
- **Copies on more devices.** **Node & trust → Keep each swarm file on** (or `--copies N`): one
  signed setting for the whole swarm keeps each swarm folder file on N devices, chosen per file
  among those that accept copies; every device works out the same choice on its own. Phones
  don't accept copies unless **Accept copies of other devices' files** is on
  (`accept_copies` under `[storage]`). Backup devices still keep everything.
- **Backed-up folders open while their device is off**: a phone's photos come from the backup
  device.
- **Network numbers**: a device's About shows the bytes it sent and received;
  `nomadfs --bench-read` and `--bench-idle` measure reads and idle traffic.
- **How it works**, a new page in the docs, and a rewritten chapter of the learning book on the
  distribution layer.

## Changed

- **Far less background traffic.** The swarm folder's listing (850 KB for a few thousand files)
  was pulled from every device every 10 seconds; now only a short fingerprint is, and the
  listing travels when it differs. An idle device went from 7–17 MB a minute to tens of KB.
- Copies are pulled by the devices that keep them; nothing is pushed when a file is saved.
  `swarm_replicas`, `replicas` and the `PUT_BLOCK` message are gone: a device stores only
  blocks it asked for.
- Lists show placeholder rows while they load, and an answer for a folder you already left no
  longer replaces the one you're on.
- The swarm folder's breadcrumb, search and sorting match Browse.

# 0.2.5 - 2026-10-29

## Added

- **About a device:** the ⓘ next to a device (and in the header while browsing it) asks it about
  itself: NomadFS version, platform, uptime, free disk space and what NomadFS uses there (kept
  blocks, read cache, quota), connections, shared folders, the swarm folder and backups, with a
  health check that flags low disk space, folders it can't read, no connections, a swarm folder
  not synced lately, and backups on mobile data. A device answers only devices it's paired with.
- **The swarm folder searches and sorts like Browse:** a search field (`/` or Ctrl+F), sortable
  Name, Size, Modified and Added by columns on wide windows and a sort menu on phones, folders
  first, the order remembered.

## Changed

- The swarm folder's breadcrumb has its own row, like Browse's; on phones **New folder** and
  **Add file** sit in a row of their own above the search.

## Fixed

- Browse: the Size header lined up left of the sizes, and folder rows were offset from file rows.

# 0.2.4 - 2026-10-29

## Fixed

- Closing the app while **Shared folders** checked where a folder is backed up could crash it.
- Quitting, or stopping a backup device, no longer waits for a whole folder copy to finish.
- Adding a file to the swarm folder with `swarm_replicas` set no longer freezes the folder,
  syncing and the app while the file is pushed to other devices.
- **File details** no longer blocks the app while it reads a file's structure from another device.
- A device could stay in the swarm after being removed, when it was added from a device whose
  clock ran ahead. Membership dated more than 10 minutes ahead is refused.
- A backup device that can't read its list of backups moves it aside instead of overwriting it;
  that list is the only record of the photos kept after the phone deleted them.
- A backed-up folder that grows past what one folder can hold (65,535 entries) keeps working:
  the files deleted on the phone move into a `Deleted before <date>` folder.
- A folder copied again only reads its index from disk, not every photo: a new photo in a
  large camera folder no longer re-reads the whole folder.
- Very deeply nested folders can't crash a backup device.
- Devices no longer resend the whole member list on every ping to a device that refused part of it.
- A device whose backup information fails still lists its shared folders.
- Two devices with the same name backing up a folder of the same name both show, told apart by
  device ID; long backup names keep whole characters and the "(backup)" ending.
- A phone sharing its mobile data as a hotspot no longer counts as being on Wi-Fi for backups.

# 0.2.3 - 2026-10-29

## Added

- **Swarm folder replication**: Configure `swarm_replicas = N` in `[storage]` or `--swarm-replicas N` to push new and edited files in the swarm folder to up to N online peers immediately upon save.
- **Settings**: Added a replica count selector for the swarm folder in the desktop and mobile settings panel.
- **Pairing with one device pairs the whole swarm.** Members vouch for each other, so a device
  paired with one member is trusted by all of them.
- **Folder backup**, like a phone's camera backup: mark a shared folder for backup (presets for
  Camera, Screenshots and WhatsApp) and every swarm backup device keeps a copy, on Wi-Fi only.
  Photos deleted on the phone stay in the backup, listed as `<device> · <folder> (backup)`.
  **Shared folders** shows where each folder is backed up.
- **File details:** the ⓘ next to a file shows its content ID, its chunks and the block tree above
  them, and a map of where this device holds each chunk.
- A shared folder that can't be read says why instead of "Scanning…" forever.

# 0.2.2 - 2026-10-29

## Fixed

- **About → Read cache** on a phone: the size has a line of its own and the controls sit below
  the text instead of squeezing it into a narrow column; the limit menu matches the app.

# 0.2.1 - 2026-10-28

## Fixed

- The one-time move of old blocks into the read cache waits for every share's first scan to
  finish, however long it takes, instead of giving up after 30 minutes. A large share still
  hashing could otherwise have had some of its own blocks moved into the cache.

# 0.2.0 - 2026-10-28

## Added

- **Read cache.** Files opened from other devices are kept in a read cache, so they open again
  without the network, and the least recently read blocks make room when it is full. It holds
  2 GB by default: `cache_mb` under `[storage]` (`0` = no limit), or the limit setting in the app.
  What a device writes, backs up or receives as a replica is kept apart and never evicted.
- **See and clear it:** **About → Read cache** in the app shows its size, with **Clear cache**;
  on the command line, `nomadfs --cache` and `nomadfs --clear-cache`.
- On first start, 0.2.0 moves the blocks it fetched before into the read cache, once, keeping
  the device's own shares and swarm folder files.

## Changed

- `quota_mb` now applies only to the blocks a device keeps; the read cache has its own limit.

# 0.1.1 - 2026-10-28

## Fixed

- **Android:** a fresh install couldn't start its node or join a swarm. The device identity
  was saved in a way Android doesn't allow apps, and a failed network accept crashed the app.
  The desktop app and the command line for ARM had the same accept bug.
- Invite and pairing codes typed on a phone are accepted when the keyboard capitalizes the
  first letter.

# 0.1.0 - 2026-10-28

A fresh start. This release breaks compatibility with every earlier build,
which were all withdrawn: **update every device and join them again**.

## Breaking

- **Handshake v2.** Connections use the standard `Noise_XXpsk3_25519_ChaChaPoly_BLAKE2s`,
  with the prologue and the ephemeral keys mixed in as the Noise spec requires, and
  checked against the published Cacophony test vector. Earlier builds can't connect.
- **New trust store (`NMDFSTR2`).** The first start of 0.1.0 moves an old trust store to
  `trust.store.v1.bak` and starts empty: pair or invite your devices again.
- **Signed revocation time.** Revocations sign when they were made (`nomadfs-revoke-v2`),
  so re-pairing a removed device is no longer undone by an older revocation.
- **UPnP is off by default**, for the command line too (`upnp_enabled = true` turns it on).
- **On Android**, uninstall the earlier app before installing 0.1.0: its version number is
  lower, so Android won't install it over the old one.

## Added

- **Notes in the swarm folder.** Text drop stores each note as a `.txt` file in the swarm's
  `Notes` folder, so every device lists every note; earlier notes move there on their own.
- **Storage quota.** `quota_mb` under `[storage]` caps the space blocks from other devices
  can take (0, the default, is unlimited).
- **Device names you can trust.** Paired devices are named after their pairing label in the
  app and the mount; unpaired devices always show a device-ID suffix and a "Not paired" badge,
  so another device can't pass itself off by copying a nickname.

## Security

- Invites: the joining device must prove it holds the identity in its ticket, the inviter
  checks expiry itself, and an older ticket can't replace a newer one.
- Revoked devices are refused under every policy, and their open connections are closed.
- Limits on what unauthenticated and authenticated peers can make a device spend:
  handshakes in flight (with a total deadline), connections, request threads, memory per
  request, block sizes, and the DAG a read may walk.
- The DHT keeps only addresses learned first-hand and drops unusable ones (loopback from
  others, port 0, multicast, link-local).
- UPnP, when on, uses a one-hour lease renewed while running and removed on stop, and only
  trusts a router on the local network.
- The app writes its configuration atomically and private (`0600`, directory `0700`).

## Fixed

- Crashes: truncating a swarm folder file after closing it; a peer sending a huge file
  size or version clock; a second invite join (double free).
- Shutdown: no use of freed memory while requests finish, no hang while discovery dials.
- The mount serves several requests at once, so one slow device no longer freezes it, and
  frees what the kernel forgets; long sessions no longer grow without bound.
- A swarm folder too large to load is moved aside instead of overwritten, and deletions are
  forgotten after 90 days. A device offline for longer may bring a deleted file back.
- Many smaller leaks and races in the trust store, the mobile library, the app and the
  storage engine.
